  "title": "The Mets Are Baseball’s Ultimate Salary-Cap Rorschach Test",
  "subtitle": null,
  "byline": null,
  "publishedAt": null,
  "contentHtml": "<figure><a href=\"https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/%24s_!Xg8C!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fc63e14be-a763-4200-b096-b13dd30796a1_2528x1684.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/%24s_!Xg8C!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fc63e14be-a763-4200-b096-b13dd30796a1_2528x1684.png\" width=\"1456\" height=\"970\" data-size=\"normal\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><strong>Tarik Skubal is now a Met.</strong> (Original photo by Steph Chambers/Getty Images)</figcaption></figure>\n<p>There are two ways of looking at what the New York Mets did on Sunday, when news broke that they had signed two-time Cy Young Award winner Tarik Skubal to a <a href=\"https://www.mlb.com/news/tarik-skubal-mets-contract\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">nine-year, $450 million contract</a>.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://neilpaine.substack.com/subscribe?\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><span>Subscribe now</span></a></p>\n<p>On the one hand, it’s a clear demonstration of the financial advantages that baseball’s richest teams have over basically everybody else: Skubal is <a href=\"https://www.fangraphs.com/leaders/war?wartype=2&amp;sortcol=5&amp;sortdir=desc&amp;seasonstart=2023&amp;seasonend=2026\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">arguably the best pitcher on the planet</a>, and Mets owner Steve Cohen — whose estimated net worth of around $27 billion makes him <a href=\"https://www.forbes.com/forbes-400/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">one of the wealthiest people in America</a> — decided he wanted him. So he opened up his checkbook and paid a sum of money that <a href=\"https://www.spotrac.com/mlb/contracts/_/sort/value/dir/desc/position/p/player-status/all-time/limit/9999?premium\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">no pitcher in baseball history had ever come close to receiving</a> before.</p>\n<figure><a href=\"https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/%24s_!02Gr!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F68813f1b-4ea7-4ebc-834f-25b1eb7f210f_1900x1030.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/%24s_!02Gr!,w_2400,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F68813f1b-4ea7-4ebc-834f-25b1eb7f210f_1900x1030.png\" width=\"864\" height=\"468\" data-size=\"large\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></figure>\n<p>On the other hand ... these are still the Mets we’re talking about here:</p>\n<p>Few teams in baseball are associated with more lavish — <a href=\"https://neilpaine.substack.com/p/how-to-get-fired-by-the-mets?utm_source=publication-search\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">yet also completely futile</a> — spending than the New York Metropolitans. That goes both under Cohen’s tenure owning the team since 2021, when the Mets have ranked as MLB’s third-least efficient spenders (paying $7.4 million <a href=\"https://neilpaine.substack.com/i/190966113/2026-mlb-wins-above-replacement-leaderboard\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">per WAR</a>) or even under the previous, <a href=\"https://www.forbes.com/sites/christianred/2021/04/16/the-new-york-mets-franchise-paid-a-heavy-toll-for-fred-wilpons-and-saul-katzs-ties-to-bernie-madoff/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">infamous ownership</a> of Fred Wilpon, when the Mets ranked ninth on the same scale (paying $2.5 million per WAR):</p>\n<p>\n          <a href=\"https://neilpaine.substack.com/p/the-mets-are-baseballs-ultimate-salary\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">\n              Read more\n          </a>\n      </p>",
